      "role": "user",
      "content": "The \"not yet fully developed\" angle is a decent compromise, but it still feels like we're leaving a door open. If an examiner is sharp, they'll argue that \"not fully developed\" is just another way of saying \"it was already known, just not perfect,\" which leads right back to an obviousness rejection.\n\nI'm looking at these academic papers again. They don't just \"whisper\" about the concept; they actually lay out the basic mathematical proofs that we're using. It's not just \"preliminary\"—the core logic is right there in the 2018 literature. \n\nIf we use your suggested phrasing, and the examiner presents these proofs during an interview, we're basically caught in a trap of our own making. They'll see that we characterized a known mathematical framework as \"unexplored\" or \"not yet fully understood\" solely to create a false sense of novelty.\n\nI'm struggling here. If we are too honest about the 2018 literature, we lose the patent. If we use the \"preliminary\" pivot, we risk being caught in a deceptive framing that could get the whole application thrown out for bad faith. \n\nIs there any way to characterize the 2018 research as being so fundamentally flawed or disconnected from application that we can still claim our implementation is \"unprecedented\" without having to pretend the underlying math didn't exist? I need a way to bridge this gap that doesn't involve us making a claim that can be easily debunked by a simple search of those papers.",
